<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gandhari-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gandhari language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gandhari Prakrit</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gandhari is a Middle Indo-Aryan language, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, of the ancient region of Gandhara in what is now north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Written in the Kharoshthi script, it was used for inscriptions, Buddhist texts and administration from the 3rd century BCE to about the 4th century CE, and spread along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, where documents at Niya record the government of Shanshan. Gandharan Buddhist texts are among the oldest Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> known.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gandhari-language.jpg" alt="Gandhari language" width="320" height="794" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuscrit_BnF_pali_715-A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Gandhari is an early Middle Indo-Aryan language with features that set it apart from every other known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>. It kept the three Old Indo-Aryan sibilants s, ś and ṣ as distinct sounds, while the other Prakrits merged them into one, and it preserved some old consonant clusters, mostly those with v and r. Intervocalic th and dh were at first written with a special letter, which suggests a sound change, probably to a voiced fricative, then to z and finally to a plain s.</p><p>Its grammar is hard to analyse, because final consonants were lost and final vowels weakened until they were no longer told apart, though a rudimentary system of case survived. The Kharoṣṭhī script does not mark long and short vowels, so how the language treated them is unknown. Some of its words, such as the term for sister, descended from Old Indo-Aryan svasṛ-, are shared with the Dardic languages, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shina-language">Shina</a> and the Kohistani languages, and Torwali stands closest to Niya Prakrit, the dialect once spoken at Niya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Gandhari appears on coins, inscriptions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. It served as an official language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> and of Central Asian kingdoms including Khotan and Shanshan, and its inscriptions have been found as far east as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. From the 1st century CE a heavily Sanskritised form of the language spread. Most Gandhari manuscripts belong to the Dharmaguptaka school, but virtually all Buddhist schools, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> included, used it to some degree. When the Kushan monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokaksema">Lokakṣema</a> began translating Buddhist sutras into Chinese in 147 CE, the earliest of these translations show signs of having been made from Gandhari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1932 E. Waldschmidt noticed that the names in the Chinese translation of the Dīrghāgama, made by the monk Buddhayaśas, were rendered not from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> but from an unidentified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> also found in the Khotan Dharmapada. In 1946 Bailey identified this language, named it Gandhari, and matched it with the language of most Kharoṣṭhī inscriptions of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Until 1994 the only known Gandhari manuscript was a birch-bark Dharmapada found at Kohmāri Mazār near Hotan in 1893. Since then many fragments have come to light in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, including 29 birch-bark scroll fragments in the British Library and palm-leaf folios in the Schøyen Collection. The corpus now counts 391 manuscripts, 901 administrative documents, mainly from Niya, and 1,234 inscriptions, besides coin legends.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gandhari language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kanishka also replaced Greek with Bactrian, written in Greek script, as the language of royal coinage and inscriptions, while <b>Gandhari Prakrit</b> in Kharosthi script and Sanskrit in Brahmi were used in India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 3rd and 4th centuries Shanshan used <b>Gandhari Prakrit</b> in Kharoshthi script for government; hundreds of wooden tablets found at Niya record taxes, lawsuits, camels and slaves.</blockquote><a href="/state/kroraina">Kroraina</a></li></ul></section>
